{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011fmxmMMNrDNMyvk7KcN8z6","type":"tool_result","content":"210\t\n211\t  Example — run 10 iterations:\n212\t    bash {{SKILL_SCRIPTS}}/afk.sh auth-system 10\n213\t```\n214\t\n215\tWhere `{{SKILL_SCRIPTS}}` is the absolute path to this skill's scripts. Resolve in this order:\n216\t\n217\t1. `~/.agents/skills/almanac/ralph-loop/scripts` — set by `almanac install codex`; use this when running in Codex.\n218\t2. `~/.claude/skills/almanac/ralph-loop/scripts` — set by `almanac install claude-code`; use this when running in Claude Code.\n219\t3. `${CLAUDE_SKILL_DIR}/scripts` — fallback if the host agent populates `CLAUDE_SKILL_DIR` from the resolved skill directory.\n220\t4. `$ALMANAC_HOME/skills/ralph-loop/scripts` — fallback when invoked outside an installed provider.\n221\t\n222\tPrint the literal provider install path in user-facing instructions (`~/.agents/...` for Codex, `~/.claude/...` for Claude Code) so users can run the scripts directly.\n223\t\n224\t## Modes\n225\t\n226\t### AFK Mode (`afk.sh`)\n227\t\n228\tFully autonomous. Runs N iterations, each in a fresh agent context. Stops when:\n229\t- All tasks complete (`<promise>COMPLETE</promise>`)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"250917c1-763a-4661-b412-670ee21b67f2","uuid":"f36cafad-f31e-4fa7-8cf9-433fdc9b4dc5","timestamp":"2026-05-26T14:20:17.940Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/skills/loop/ralph-loop/SKILL.md","content":"\n  Example — run 10 iterations:\n    bash {{SKILL_SCRIPTS}}/afk.sh auth-system 10\n```\n\nWhere `{{SKILL_SCRIPTS}}` is the absolute path to this skill's scripts. Resolve in this order:\n\n1. `~/.agents/skills/almanac/ralph-loop/scripts` — set by `almanac install codex`; use this when running in Codex.\n2. `~/.claude/skills/almanac/ralph-loop/scripts` — set by `almanac install claude-code`; use this when running in Claude Code.\n3. `${CLAUDE_SKILL_DIR}/scripts` — fallback if the host agent populates `CLAUDE_SKILL_DIR` from the resolved skill directory.\n4. `$ALMANAC_HOME/skills/ralph-loop/scripts` — fallback when invoked outside an installed provider.\n\nPrint the literal provider install path in user-facing instructions (`~/.agents/...` for Codex, `~/.claude/...` for Claude Code) so users can run the scripts directly.\n\n## Modes\n\n### AFK Mode (`afk.sh`)\n\nFully autonomous. Runs N iterations, each in a fresh agent context. Stops when:\n- All tasks complete (`<promise>COMPLETE</promise>`)","numLines":20,"startLine":210,"totalLines":300}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01MYgZwgeSjFxZGEgynmXTet","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014pSRmVN8eostcX39SEaNPo","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/skills/other/tdd/SKILL.md","offset":15,"limit":85},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":752,"cache_read_input_tokens":54276,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":752},"output_tokens":50,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"250917c1-763a-4661-b412-670ee21b67f2","uuid":"963f4ba9-7c31-424b-80b5-02f5e737da30","request_id":"req_011CbRKtx618ixUV2cVZgyx8"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014pSRmVN8eostcX39SEaNPo","type":"tool_result","content":"15\t\n16\t**Good tests** are integration-style: they exercise real code paths through public APIs. They describe _what_ the system does, not _how_ it does it. A good test reads like a specification - \"user can checkout with valid cart\" tells you exactly what capability exists. These tests survive refactors because they don't care about internal structure.\n17\t\n18\t**Bad tests** are coupled to implementation. They mock internal collaborators, test private methods, or verify through external means (like querying a database directly instead of using the interface). The warning sign: your test breaks when you refactor, but behavior hasn't changed. If you rename an internal function and tests fail, those tests were testing implementation, not behavior.\n19\t\n20\tSee `${CLAUDE_SKILL_DIR}/references/tests.md` for examples and `${CLAUDE_SKILL_DIR}/references/mocking.md` for mocking guidelines.\n21\t\n22\t## Anti-Pattern: Horizontal Slices\n23\t\n24\t**DO NOT write all tests first, then all implementation.** This is \"horizontal slicing\" - treating RED as \"write all tests\" and GREEN as \"write all code.\"\n25\t\n26\tThis produces **crap tests**:\n27\t\n28\t- Tests written in bulk test _imagined_ behavior, not _actual_ behavior\n29\t- You end up testing the _shape_ of things (data structures, function signatures) rather than user-facing behavior\n30\t- Tests become insensitive to real changes - they pass when behavior breaks, fail when behavior is fine\n31\t- You outrun your headlights, committing to test structure before understanding the implementation\n32\t\n33\t**Correct approach**: Vertical slices via tracer bullets. One test -> one implementation -> repeat. Each test responds to what you learned from the previous cycle. Because you just wrote the code, you know exactly what behavior matters and how to verify it.\n34\t\n35\t```\n36\tWRONG (horizontal):\n37\t  RED:   test1, test2, test3, test4, test5\n38\t  GREEN: impl1, impl2, impl3, impl4, impl5\n39\t\n40\tRIGHT (vertical):\n41\t  RED->GREEN: test1->impl1\n42\t  RED->GREEN: test2->impl2\n43\t  RED->GREEN: test3->impl3\n44\t  ...\n45\t```\n46\t\n47\t## Workflow\n48\t\n49\t### 1. Planning\n50\t\n51\tWhen exploring the codebase, use `CONTEXT.md` vocabulary so test names + interface match the project's language; respect ADRs in the area you touch.\n52\t\n53\tBefore writing any code:\n54\t\n55\t- [ ] Confirm with user what interface changes are needed\n56\t- [ ] Confirm with user which behaviors to test (prioritize)\n57\t- [ ] Identify opportunities for deep modules — see `${CLAUDE_SKILL_DIR}/references/deep-modules.md`\n58\t- [ ] Design interfaces for testability — see `${CLAUDE_SKILL_DIR}/references/interface-design.md`\n59\t- [ ] List the behaviors to test (not implementation steps)\n60\t- [ ] Get user approval on the plan\n61\t\n62\tAsk: \"What should the public interface look like? Which behaviors are most important to test?\"\n63\t\n64\t**You can't test everything.** Confirm with the user exactly which behaviors matter most. Focus testing effort on critical paths and complex logic, not every possible edge case.\n65\t\n66\t### 2. Tracer Bullet\n67\t\n68\tWrite ONE test that confirms ONE thing about the system:\n69\t\n70\t```\n71\tRED:   Write test for first behavior -> test fails\n72\tGREEN: Write minimal code to pass -> test passes\n73\t```\n74\t\n75\tThis is your tracer bullet - proves the path works end-to-end.\n76\t\n77\t### 3. Incremental Loop\n78\t\n79\tFor each remaining behavior:\n80\t\n81\t```\n82\tRED:   Write next test -> fails\n83\tGREEN: Minimal code to pass -> passes\n84\t```\n85\t\n86\tRules:\n87\t\n88\t- One test at a time\n89\t- Only enough code to pass current test\n90\t- Don't anticipate future tests\n91\t- Keep tests focused on observable behavior\n92\t\n93\t### 4. Refactor\n94\t\n95\tAfter all tests pass, look for refactor candidates — see `${CLAUDE_SKILL_DIR}/references/refactoring.md`:\n96\t\n97\t- [ ] Extract duplication\n98\t- [ ] Deepen modules (move complexity behind simple interfaces)\n99\t- [ ] Apply SOLID principles where natural"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"250917c1-763a-4661-b412-670ee21b67f2","uuid":"72c72fa3-228c-4b36-9132-e5a6bd0dd1e8","timestamp":"2026-05-26T14:20:18.995Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/skills/other/tdd/SKILL.md","content":"\n**Good tests** are integration-style: they exercise real code paths through public APIs. They describe _what_ the system does, not _how_ it does it. A good test reads like a specification - \"user can checkout with valid cart\" tells you exactly what capability exists. These tests survive refactors because they don't care about internal structure.\n\n**Bad tests** are coupled to implementation. They mock internal collaborators, test private methods, or verify through external means (like querying a database directly instead of using the interface). The warning sign: your test breaks when you refactor, but behavior hasn't changed. If you rename an internal function and tests fail, those tests were testing implementation, not behavior.\n\nSee `${CLAUDE_SKILL_DIR}/references/tests.md` for examples and `${CLAUDE_SKILL_DIR}/references/mocking.md` for mocking guidelines.\n\n## Anti-Pattern: Horizontal Slices\n\n**DO NOT write all tests first, then all implementation.** This is \"horizontal slicing\" - treating RED as \"write all tests\" and GREEN as \"write all code.\"\n\nThis produces **crap tests**:\n\n- Tests written in bulk test _imagined_ behavior, not _actual_ behavior\n- You end up testing the _shape_ of things (data structures, function signatures) rather than user-facing behavior\n- Tests become insensitive to real changes - they pass when behavior breaks, fail when behavior is fine\n- You outrun your headlights, committing to test structure before understanding the implementation\n\n**Correct approach**: Vertical slices via tracer bullets. One test -> one implementation -> repeat. Each test responds to what you learned from the previous cycle. Because you just wrote the code, you know exactly what behavior matters and how to verify it.\n\n```\nWRONG (horizontal):\n  RED:   test1, test2, test3, test4, test5\n  GREEN: impl1, impl2, impl3, impl4, impl5\n\nRIGHT (vertical):\n  RED->GREEN: test1->impl1\n  RED->GREEN: test2->impl2\n  RED->GREEN: test3->impl3\n  ...\n```\n\n## Workflow\n\n### 1. Planning\n\nWhen exploring the codebase, use `CONTEXT.md` vocabulary so test names + interface match the project's language; respect ADRs in the area you touch.\n\nBefore writing any code:\n\n- [ ] Confirm with user what interface changes are needed\n- [ ] Confirm with user which behaviors to test (prioritize)\n- [ ] Identify opportunities for deep modules — see `${CLAUDE_SKILL_DIR}/references/deep-modules.md`\n- [ ] Design interfaces for testability — see `${CLAUDE_SKILL_DIR}/references/interface-design.md`\n- [ ] List the behaviors to test (not implementation steps)\n- [ ] Get user approval on the plan\n\nAsk: \"What should the public interface look like? Which behaviors are most important to test?\"\n\n**You can't test everything.** Confirm with the user exactly which behaviors matter most. Focus testing effort on critical paths and complex logic, not every possible edge case.\n\n### 2. Tracer Bullet\n\nWrite ONE test that confirms ONE thing about the system:\n\n```\nRED:   Write test for first behavior -> test fails\nGREEN: Write minimal code to pass -> test passes\n```\n\nThis is your tracer bullet - proves the path works end-to-end.\n\n### 3. Incremental Loop\n\nFor each remaining behavior:\n\n```\nRED:   Write next test -> fails\nGREEN: Minimal code to pass -> passes\n```\n\nRules:\n\n- One test at a time\n- Only enough code to pass current test\n- Don't anticipate future tests\n- Keep tests focused on observable behavior\n\n### 4. Refactor\n\nAfter all tests pass, look for refactor candidates — see `${CLAUDE_SKILL_DIR}/references/refactoring.md`:\n\n- [ ] Extract duplication\n- [ ] Deepen modules (move complexity behind simple interfaces)\n- [ ] Apply SOLID principles where natural","numLines":85,"startLine":15,"totalLines":114}}}
